1
00:00:06,256 --> 00:00:07,966
Okay, so you made it.

2
00:00:08,174 --> 00:00:10,510
Now you need a car that fits your life,

3
00:00:10,593 --> 00:00:12,345
but you're not a real showy person.

4
00:00:12,429 --> 00:00:13,805
You're a quality person.

5
00:00:14,097 --> 00:00:15,598
What are you going to drive?

6
00:00:16,224 --> 00:00:17,517
You drive this!

7
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:18,518
[rock music plays]

8
00:00:18,601 --> 00:00:19,853
[engine revs]

9
00:00:21,104 --> 00:00:25,442
This is a 1983 Mercedes-Benz
300 turbo diesel station wagon

10
00:00:25,775 --> 00:00:27,068
in mango green.

11
00:00:27,152 --> 00:00:28,611
No, mango green!

12
00:00:28,695 --> 00:00:30,280
-Let's bring her out.
-[engine revs]

13
00:00:30,363 --> 00:00:32,907
It has a three-liter ,
six-cylinder diesel engine,

14
00:00:32,991 --> 00:00:37,245
with a four-speed manual 
that makes 77 horsepower with turbo.

15
00:00:38,580 --> 00:00:41,082
It has a famously indestructible 
diesel engine.

16
00:00:41,166 --> 00:00:43,668
You could probably pee in the tank 
and it would keep going.

17
00:00:44,294 --> 00:00:47,422
The other thing I love about this car?
The color. The color.

18
00:00:47,505 --> 00:00:50,633
The human eye perceives color 
through cones in the retina.

19
00:00:50,717 --> 00:00:52,802
This color hits my cones!

20
00:00:53,803 --> 00:00:54,929
They do last forever.

21
00:00:55,013 --> 00:00:59,517
A Greek cab driver drove one of these
4.6 million kilometers.

22
00:00:59,601 --> 00:01:01,394
Gregorios Sachinidis.

23
00:01:01,603 --> 00:01:04,397
He got out, put on this beautiful suit,
and then just kept going!

24
00:01:05,231 --> 00:01:08,359
♪ Remember times back in the '80s ♪

25
00:01:08,443 --> 00:01:12,655
♪ When I was still young
And rolled a Hot Wheels Mercedes  ♪

26
00:01:13,406 --> 00:01:15,283
[Jerry] They have a beautiful
cloud-like ride.

27
00:01:15,366 --> 00:01:16,951
You just feel safe and secure.

28
00:01:17,285 --> 00:01:18,119
[laughs]

29
00:01:18,578 --> 00:01:21,790
And in comedy,
we never feel safe and secure,

30
00:01:21,873 --> 00:01:25,543
unless we are working with someone 
like my very special guest today...

31
00:01:25,627 --> 00:01:28,421
Why are streets so wide?

32
00:01:28,505 --> 00:01:29,714
[Jerry] ...Mr. Martin Short.

33
00:01:29,798 --> 00:01:32,050
When Marty Short is coming on your show...

34
00:01:32,467 --> 00:01:34,677
[Martin] Why is Uber so popular? 

35
00:01:34,761 --> 00:01:36,095
[Jerry] ...you are relaxed,

36
00:01:36,179 --> 00:01:39,015
because you know
you're gonna get funny stuff. 

37
00:01:39,098 --> 00:01:40,683
-[imitates Martin] Why?
-[laughs]

38
00:01:40,767 --> 00:01:41,768
[cell phone rings]

39
00:01:41,851 --> 00:01:43,895
-[Martin] Hello?
-[Jerry] Marty, it's Jerry.

40
00:01:43,978 --> 00:01:46,981
[Martin] Oh, Jerry, yeah.
I'm just sleeping here. How you doing?

41
00:01:47,065 --> 00:01:49,067
[Jerry] Good. Did you forget 
we have a show tonight?

42
00:01:49,150 --> 00:01:51,194
[Martin] Um, I guess I did.

43
00:01:51,277 --> 00:01:52,862
[Jerry] Yeah. It's Tuesday.

44
00:01:52,946 --> 00:01:54,739
-[Martin] Oh.
-I'm on my way to your house.

45
00:01:54,823 --> 00:01:58,159
[Martin] Oh! I have a spray 
that can put me back together again.

46
00:02:02,455 --> 00:02:03,915
[Jerry] So excited to see him today.

47
00:02:03,998 --> 00:02:06,292
And that's why I am Jerry Seinfeld,

48
00:02:06,376 --> 00:02:10,046
and this
is Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

49
00:02:13,842 --> 00:02:14,676
[knocking]

50
00:02:14,759 --> 00:02:15,802
Do you live here?

51
00:02:15,885 --> 00:02:19,305
Yes, this is my home. 
We bought it in 1986.

52
00:02:19,389 --> 00:02:21,057
-At the peak.
-At the p-- [laughs]

53
00:02:21,141 --> 00:02:23,518
-Yeah, the absolute peak.
-Yeah, the short peak.

54
00:02:25,228 --> 00:02:26,938
First of all, look at this car.
First of all.

55
00:02:27,021 --> 00:02:29,482
First of all, yeah,
this is the car I picked for you.

56
00:02:29,566 --> 00:02:30,567
It's beautiful.

57
00:02:30,650 --> 00:02:34,070
Nobody is nervous on the day
Marty's booked on the show.

58
00:02:34,487 --> 00:02:35,905
No one's nervous that day.

59
00:02:35,989 --> 00:02:37,907
Every other day, they are.

60
00:02:37,991 --> 00:02:40,869
So how do you translate
that sense of security into a car?

61
00:02:43,121 --> 00:02:44,330
Have you had coffee yet?

62
00:02:44,414 --> 00:02:46,291
-Uh, no. I'm dying for coffee.
-Great. Let's go.

63
00:02:50,461 --> 00:02:52,338
[Jerry] Where is Jiminy Glick?
Is he coming back?

64
00:02:52,422 --> 00:02:54,799
I'm one of the people...
I'm gonna give myself a compliment.

65
00:02:54,883 --> 00:02:56,885
-You were the first Jiminy.
-No way.

66
00:02:56,968 --> 00:02:59,429
You were the two first Jiminys.
Do you not realize that?

67
00:02:59,512 --> 00:03:00,346
No.

68
00:03:00,430 --> 00:03:02,265
When I did Primetime Glick
on Comedy Central,

69
00:03:02,348 --> 00:03:03,808
and you were the first guest.

70
00:03:04,100 --> 00:03:05,185
-Really?
-Yes, sir.

71
00:03:05,268 --> 00:03:08,396
I've never seen a shirt collar
that actually goes up like that.

72
00:03:08,730 --> 00:03:12,233
I mean, the distance
from the top of your shirt to your belt

73
00:03:12,317 --> 00:03:13,443
is three inches.

74
00:03:13,526 --> 00:03:15,111
I'll tell you why, it's Pilates.

75
00:03:16,029 --> 00:03:17,989
It was always a thrill for me.

76
00:03:18,072 --> 00:03:19,407
-It was my favorite...
-Oh, my God.

77
00:03:19,490 --> 00:03:22,285
'Cause when you go on with Jiminy Glick,
you can say anything you want.

78
00:03:22,660 --> 00:03:24,495
Um, Dave Foley said to me,

79
00:03:24,579 --> 00:03:27,457
"You've finally created  a character
who's as mean as you really are."

80
00:03:27,540 --> 00:03:28,374
[laughs]

81
00:03:42,472 --> 00:03:45,016
-You want to order food?
-I'll sip coffee for a while.

82
00:03:48,978 --> 00:03:51,564
So, you're starting out,
you're doing stand-up, 

83
00:03:51,648 --> 00:03:53,858
before the pilot, before '89,

84
00:03:53,942 --> 00:03:55,109
what's in your mind?

85
00:03:55,193 --> 00:03:59,781
Accomplishment to be creatively successful
or fame?

86
00:03:59,864 --> 00:04:01,282
Like, what's motivating you?

87
00:04:01,449 --> 00:04:03,701
Well, why would you separate them,
first of all?

88
00:04:03,785 --> 00:04:05,536
-I think--
-You're in show business.

89
00:04:05,662 --> 00:04:06,496
I think it's like--

90
00:04:06,579 --> 00:04:09,707
It's like saying, "I wanna be a banker,
be really successful, but not with money."

91
00:04:09,791 --> 00:04:11,125
I don't... I mean, like...

92
00:04:11,542 --> 00:04:13,378
[both laugh]

93
00:04:16,381 --> 00:04:19,884
But I remember, you know, 
and this is now in the early '70s,

94
00:04:20,134 --> 00:04:23,263
before she did SNL, Gilda Radner 
and I were boyfriend-girlfriend.

95
00:04:23,346 --> 00:04:24,681
-I didn't know that!
-Yeah.

96
00:04:24,764 --> 00:04:27,433
For a couple of years.
And in our group, Paul Shaffer.

97
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:29,018
We all did this show, Godspell,

98
00:04:29,102 --> 00:04:32,438
and Paul was the first person in our group
that went down New York.

99
00:04:32,522 --> 00:04:34,232
He was gonna play The Magic Show.

100
00:04:34,315 --> 00:04:35,608
-I remember being--
-Doug Henning?

101
00:04:35,692 --> 00:04:40,280
Yeah. "The magic is illusion.
And illusion can be magical!"

102
00:04:40,363 --> 00:04:41,239
For that act.

103
00:04:41,906 --> 00:04:46,786
I remember Gilda and I phoning Paul,
and we were on extensions.

104
00:04:47,287 --> 00:04:50,206
And Gilda saying,
"Paul, what are New York actors like?"

105
00:04:51,165 --> 00:04:52,083
And Paul saying, 

106
00:04:52,625 --> 00:04:56,296
"I don't know, maybe I'm prejudiced,
but I think you guys are just as good."

107
00:04:56,379 --> 00:04:58,298
-Right.
-And then I remember we got off the phone,

108
00:04:58,381 --> 00:05:01,718
we both went, "Ah! He's so sweet."
We didn't buy it. We didn't believe him.

109
00:05:01,801 --> 00:05:03,344
Right. What year would that be?

110
00:05:03,428 --> 00:05:04,554
Seventy-three.

111
00:05:04,637 --> 00:05:08,224
And this show, Godspell, came to Toronto. 
And everyone wanted it.

112
00:05:08,308 --> 00:05:10,560
It was gonna be a year,
and it was a hit in New York.

113
00:05:10,643 --> 00:05:14,105
And they whittled it down
probably from 500 people to ten.

114
00:05:14,313 --> 00:05:16,190
And then, that ten was Eugene Levy...

115
00:05:16,899 --> 00:05:18,026
and Andrea Martin,

116
00:05:18,609 --> 00:05:19,736
and Victor Garber...

117
00:05:20,361 --> 00:05:22,322
and myself, and Gilda.

118
00:05:23,656 --> 00:05:25,658
-I'll have some scrambled eggs.
-Some scrambled eggs.

119
00:05:25,742 --> 00:05:26,826
Whole wheat toast.

120
00:05:27,076 --> 00:05:28,911
We have sourdough or olive toast.

121
00:05:31,205 --> 00:05:32,415
[Jerry laughs]

122
00:05:33,666 --> 00:05:35,251
-Sourdough.
-Wonderful.

123
00:05:35,335 --> 00:05:39,130
You just had an aneurysm there on...
when you heard "olive toast."

124
00:05:41,132 --> 00:05:43,634
I remember my mother 
when she got really old.

125
00:05:43,718 --> 00:05:47,055
That a waiter would ask her what she
wanted would always catch her by surprise.

126
00:05:47,138 --> 00:05:49,098
And then, she would go, "Oh."

127
00:05:49,599 --> 00:05:51,601
[laughs]

128
00:05:54,103 --> 00:05:55,897
I tend to, I must tell you,

129
00:05:56,147 --> 00:06:00,985
with funny people, I am so delighted
with something that's been said funny

130
00:06:01,486 --> 00:06:05,114
that I will immediately say, "Hey!
You know what Larry just said?" I'll go--

131
00:06:05,198 --> 00:06:07,492
-Like, he'll say something really funny.
-Yeah.

132
00:06:07,575 --> 00:06:09,744
They'll say it to me,
I'll immediately tell three people.

133
00:06:10,078 --> 00:06:14,415
Because a funny line that is clever is
the most exhilarating thing in the world.

134
00:06:14,499 --> 00:06:15,583
There you have it.

135
00:06:17,502 --> 00:06:18,753
We have this in our show now.

136
00:06:19,128 --> 00:06:22,215
Steve quotes this line from Bob Newhart,
which he says,

137
00:06:22,715 --> 00:06:26,094
"I don't mean to denigrate
anyone who likes country music.

138
00:06:26,469 --> 00:06:29,555
And for people who like country music,
'denigrate' means 'put down'."

139
00:06:29,639 --> 00:06:30,473
[laughs]

140
00:06:30,556 --> 00:06:33,726
And the audience laughs as hard

141
00:06:34,060 --> 00:06:36,604
as they laugh at anything in the night.

142
00:06:36,687 --> 00:06:37,563
And this is...

143
00:06:38,314 --> 00:06:40,733
just a great joke from Bob Newhart.

144
00:06:41,025 --> 00:06:41,901
He does it--

145
00:06:42,151 --> 00:06:45,613
What we do is we do a little tribute 
to great comedians and their great lines.

146
00:06:45,696 --> 00:06:46,614
Am I in it?

147
00:06:46,697 --> 00:06:48,199
No. And I wish you were.

148
00:06:49,867 --> 00:06:54,122
If I had to say, "There's a picture
of Jerry and one of his best jokes,"

149
00:06:54,205 --> 00:06:56,582
what would that joke be? Or best line.

150
00:06:57,083 --> 00:06:57,917
Uh...

151
00:06:58,626 --> 00:07:00,378
That's why you're not in it. [laughs]

152
00:07:00,461 --> 00:07:01,838
[laughing]

153
00:07:04,841 --> 00:07:06,426
And then, Second City Chicago

154
00:07:06,551 --> 00:07:08,970
formed a sister company in the spring.
Now, it's '73.

155
00:07:09,470 --> 00:07:12,223
Gilda, she gets in. Eugene Levy gets in.

156
00:07:12,306 --> 00:07:15,268
Danny Aykroyd wasn't in the cast, 
but he was our friend. He gets in.

157
00:07:15,476 --> 00:07:17,478
John Candy gets in.

158
00:07:18,354 --> 00:07:19,522
And I never auditioned.

159
00:07:19,605 --> 00:07:20,898
'Cause I would be, like, parties.

160
00:07:20,982 --> 00:07:22,650
-I was the funny guy.
-Yeah.

161
00:07:22,733 --> 00:07:25,570
I mean, everybody was funny,
but I was certainly not passive.

162
00:07:26,529 --> 00:07:28,739
And, you know,
I had to think about it later,

163
00:07:28,823 --> 00:07:31,117
but I would say, "No, I'm an actor.
I don't want to improv,"

164
00:07:31,200 --> 00:07:33,953
'cause I didn't want to be funny
on demand like that.

165
00:07:34,036 --> 00:07:34,871
Ah.

166
00:07:34,954 --> 00:07:37,165
And it was after I realized
the error of my ways,

167
00:07:37,248 --> 00:07:39,250
a couple years later
that I joined Second City.

168
00:07:41,043 --> 00:07:43,129
You know, nature decides.

169
00:07:43,504 --> 00:07:45,339
That talent, that's like a...

170
00:07:45,423 --> 00:07:48,342
You know, it's like those plants 
that come through the cement.

171
00:07:48,426 --> 00:07:49,760
-Like the movie Alien.
-Mm-hmm.

172
00:07:49,844 --> 00:07:52,889
It decides, "I'm coming out 
of this guy's chest in this scene

173
00:07:52,972 --> 00:07:54,348
whether he likes it or not."

174
00:07:54,849 --> 00:07:55,850
-Right?
-Yes.

175
00:07:57,351 --> 00:07:58,436
[Hannah] More coffee here?

176
00:07:58,519 --> 00:08:00,605
Hannah, I would have more coffee
and maybe

177
00:08:01,189 --> 00:08:02,315
a lovely orange juice.

178
00:08:02,398 --> 00:08:03,357
-You got it.
-Thank you.

179
00:08:03,441 --> 00:08:04,984
That's a great idea. I'll have one, too.

180
00:08:05,067 --> 00:08:07,320
Orange juice? I'll be back
with some more coffee for you.

181
00:08:07,403 --> 00:08:10,072
-You know what, take the flowers.
-Oh! I am taking the flowers.

182
00:08:12,867 --> 00:08:17,788
How do you know when you've lost it 
and shouldn't keep doing it?

183
00:08:17,872 --> 00:08:19,498
No one tells you the truth.

184
00:08:19,582 --> 00:08:21,667
Your agent doesn't tell you,
your lawyer doesn't tell,

185
00:08:21,751 --> 00:08:22,960
your wife doesn't tell you.

186
00:08:23,044 --> 00:08:26,297
So there's about a five-year period
where you should've stopped...

187
00:08:26,714 --> 00:08:28,633
but you keep still doing it.

188
00:08:28,716 --> 00:08:31,844
It's like Lucille Ball was... 
I Love Lucy. Genius.

189
00:08:31,928 --> 00:08:34,305
-Right.
-The Lucy Show. Okay.

190
00:08:35,223 --> 00:08:36,557
[both laugh]

191
00:08:36,974 --> 00:08:40,436
You know? I mean, it's just not...
it's not as good.

192
00:08:40,520 --> 00:08:41,354
[both laughing]

193
00:08:42,438 --> 00:08:45,191
-Wait. There's one more.
-Then there's Here's Lucy.

194
00:08:45,441 --> 00:08:46,692
Now we're in trouble.

195
00:08:46,776 --> 00:08:47,693
[raspy voice] Help!

196
00:08:47,777 --> 00:08:49,195
Voice is shot, you know.

197
00:08:51,447 --> 00:08:54,242
For a long time,
I could pull off this bit.

198
00:08:54,325 --> 00:08:55,701
So, I'm gonna walk over to you.

199
00:08:55,785 --> 00:08:57,620
-You say, "Oh, young man!"
-Oh, young man.

200
00:08:58,120 --> 00:08:59,413
[Jerry laughing]

201
00:08:59,497 --> 00:09:00,331
Yes?

202
00:09:00,831 --> 00:09:02,083
Did someone call me?

203
00:09:02,416 --> 00:09:03,834
But now, you can't do it.

204
00:09:03,918 --> 00:09:06,921
You could still do that bit. 
I wouldn't use "young man."

205
00:09:07,004 --> 00:09:08,714
There's something else.
I would change that.

206
00:09:08,798 --> 00:09:10,716
Well, or the argument could be 

207
00:09:10,800 --> 00:09:14,178
that you keep "young man"
because it's so clearly isn't a young man.

208
00:09:14,262 --> 00:09:15,096
Yes.

209
00:09:16,222 --> 00:09:19,809
As I said, never assuming
that the kind of fame that I'd see

210
00:09:19,892 --> 00:09:22,228
Jerry Lewis walking out on a stage
of The Tonight Show,

211
00:09:22,311 --> 00:09:23,354
that wasn't gonna be me.

212
00:09:23,437 --> 00:09:25,189
What did you get to do with him?

213
00:09:25,273 --> 00:09:28,192
-That was great.
-I've impersonated him on SCTV.

214
00:09:29,110 --> 00:09:31,487
It was very important
that I made Jerry really funny,

215
00:09:31,571 --> 00:09:35,157
'cause I think that's kind of the secret
of when you satirize something.

216
00:09:35,241 --> 00:09:37,076
[big band jazz plays]

217
00:09:37,577 --> 00:09:39,829
And sometimes that ingredient
is not included,

218
00:09:39,912 --> 00:09:42,039
and it's a big, big mistake, which is,

219
00:09:42,123 --> 00:09:45,334
if you're satirizing Jerry Lewis, 
it's like a Hirschfeld sketch.

220
00:09:45,418 --> 00:09:47,503
You can show the warts and the arrogance,

221
00:09:47,837 --> 00:09:50,464
but you also have to show the genius

222
00:09:50,715 --> 00:09:53,926
and why people looked at him 
and loved him to begin with.

223
00:09:54,010 --> 00:09:56,262
-Yeah.
-Then when I met him I was very nervous.

224
00:09:56,345 --> 00:09:57,263
Oh, yeah.

225
00:09:57,346 --> 00:10:01,559
And he was so sweet and so lovely 
and great with me.

226
00:10:01,642 --> 00:10:02,977
And he'd seen your stuff?

227
00:10:03,060 --> 00:10:05,229
Later on, he said,
"Didn't you do me on the telephone?"

228
00:10:05,313 --> 00:10:06,564
And he went... [laughs]

229
00:10:07,064 --> 00:10:08,107
"That sounds funny."

230
00:10:08,190 --> 00:10:09,317
-Really?
-[Martin] Yeah.

231
00:10:09,400 --> 00:10:11,235
-He was lovely. He was lovely.
-Wow. Yeah.

232
00:10:14,322 --> 00:10:16,032
Stand-up was not, um...

233
00:10:16,115 --> 00:10:17,491
I tried it once,

234
00:10:17,992 --> 00:10:20,828
when I just first left Second City stage.

235
00:10:20,911 --> 00:10:24,915
And a friend of mine, Carole Pope,
had a punk rock group called Rough Trade.

236
00:10:25,291 --> 00:10:27,460
And she said, 
"Why don't you open it for me?"

237
00:10:27,668 --> 00:10:29,253
And I said, "Sure."

238
00:10:29,795 --> 00:10:32,131
It was on a Monday.
So I thought, "I'll write some material."

239
00:10:32,632 --> 00:10:33,507
And I went out,

240
00:10:33,591 --> 00:10:35,259
and the whole audience, you know, had

241
00:10:35,551 --> 00:10:38,804
"Rough Trade" carved in their neck,
and they were in chaps, and...

242
00:10:38,971 --> 00:10:40,973
You know, two were dressed
as my deceased parents.

243
00:10:41,057 --> 00:10:43,017
I mean, it was a horrible audience.

244
00:10:43,476 --> 00:10:44,685
And, um...

245
00:10:44,769 --> 00:10:47,730
And my material was more like 
not so much that it was joke,

246
00:10:47,813 --> 00:10:51,359
but people would turn to each other
and go, "Exactly." You know?

247
00:10:51,442 --> 00:10:54,528
And it was not the crowd.
And a guy through a beer in my face.

248
00:10:54,612 --> 00:10:56,530
After that, Carole came
to my dressing room.

249
00:10:56,614 --> 00:10:58,741
She said, "I'm so sorry
that that happened.

250
00:10:58,824 --> 00:11:00,368
Tomorrow night's gonna be so different."

251
00:11:00,451 --> 00:11:03,162
And I said, "Yes, it will be
because I'll be home watching Mannix."

252
00:11:05,331 --> 00:11:08,584
I think one of the reasons Steve Martin
is enjoying what we're doing so much 

253
00:11:08,668 --> 00:11:10,795
is that he'll have such belief

254
00:11:10,878 --> 00:11:12,088
-in a new line.
-Yes.

255
00:11:12,171 --> 00:11:15,883
And he'll literally paste things,
and, "I'm so excited about tonight!"

256
00:11:15,966 --> 00:11:17,551
-Yes.
-"I want to say that new line.

257
00:11:17,635 --> 00:11:19,553
-I just feel it's gonna work."
-Yeah. [laugh]

258
00:11:19,637 --> 00:11:21,806
And then, it gets nothing.

259
00:11:21,889 --> 00:11:22,723
Nothing.

260
00:11:23,140 --> 00:11:24,350
And I stare at him.

261
00:11:24,934 --> 00:11:28,312
And the two of us
go completely hysterical, right?

262
00:11:28,646 --> 00:11:32,358
And then, after the show, we're drinking
a glass of wine and we are loving

263
00:11:32,441 --> 00:11:33,943
-this failure.
-Yes.

264
00:11:36,821 --> 00:11:39,490
Like, I always feel so badly 
for these reality television people,

265
00:11:39,573 --> 00:11:41,409
because show business is so hard anyway.

266
00:11:41,492 --> 00:11:43,994
Can you imagine embarking on it
with zero talent?

267
00:11:44,078 --> 00:11:47,456
First of all, they're grateful
for the half second they're gonna get.

268
00:11:47,540 --> 00:11:49,166
-I guess so, I guess so.
-And...

269
00:11:49,250 --> 00:11:51,627
they don't know,
as they're paddling the canoe,

270
00:11:51,711 --> 00:11:53,713
that what that rumbling sound

271
00:11:53,796 --> 00:11:55,172
a hundred yards away is.

272
00:11:55,256 --> 00:11:56,507
-Like Deliverance.
-Uh-huh.

273
00:11:56,590 --> 00:11:59,051
They don't know 
that they're about to go over the falls.

274
00:11:59,135 --> 00:11:59,969
Mm-hmm.

275
00:12:00,344 --> 00:12:01,595
[laughing]

276
00:12:02,972 --> 00:12:03,806
And it's all right.

277
00:12:03,889 --> 00:12:05,683
They're just going through
all the Deliverance.

278
00:12:05,766 --> 00:12:08,269
Yeah, They're just, you know,
"Hey! This is fantastic.

279
00:12:08,352 --> 00:12:09,186
What is that sound?"

280
00:12:09,270 --> 00:12:10,730
"I don't know, but this is great."

281
00:12:14,150 --> 00:12:16,235
When you're going in
to socialize with people

282
00:12:16,318 --> 00:12:18,612
who are not in show business in any way...

283
00:12:18,696 --> 00:12:21,991
Well, I raised my kids, you know,
in Pacific Palisades.

284
00:12:22,074 --> 00:12:24,160
We raised them with a bunch of families,
you know,

285
00:12:24,243 --> 00:12:25,661
who also have kids of similar ages.

286
00:12:25,745 --> 00:12:28,330
And after 25 years,

287
00:12:28,414 --> 00:12:30,541
you've known them for 25 years,
these people.

288
00:12:30,624 --> 00:12:31,542
And they're great.

289
00:12:32,168 --> 00:12:33,878
-And...
-Are they? Are they really?

290
00:12:34,462 --> 00:12:35,671
[laughing]

291
00:12:38,674 --> 00:12:42,303
-It's like when people come backstage.
-How about when they don't say anything?

292
00:12:42,386 --> 00:12:44,096
-Happens to me all the time.
-All the time.

293
00:12:44,180 --> 00:12:46,932
I once had a bunch of friends

294
00:12:47,016 --> 00:12:48,517
who are not in show business

295
00:12:48,601 --> 00:12:49,685
to come backstage.

296
00:12:49,769 --> 00:12:52,855
They do not mention the show.

297
00:12:52,938 --> 00:12:54,398
Then we go to dinner,

298
00:12:54,732 --> 00:12:57,776
and they do not mention the show.

299
00:12:57,860 --> 00:13:02,114
And then the bill comes 
and I grab it and I pay for it.

300
00:13:02,406 --> 00:13:04,325
And then now we're heading back home,

301
00:13:04,408 --> 00:13:06,827
and my wife, Nancy, and I
have an argument.

302
00:13:06,911 --> 00:13:08,329
"Why would you pay that bill?

303
00:13:08,412 --> 00:13:09,622
It was eight people."

304
00:13:09,705 --> 00:13:12,208
I said,
"Because I had to get out of there.

305
00:13:12,291 --> 00:13:16,253
Because no one had mentioned a word
about something I'm just

306
00:13:16,337 --> 00:13:18,964
still sweating from doing 
for two and a half hours.

307
00:13:19,048 --> 00:13:20,466
-Get me out of there!"
-[laughs]

308
00:13:20,549 --> 00:13:22,384
You know, to me,
if you're in show business,

309
00:13:22,468 --> 00:13:24,845
you come back and say, "That was fun."

310
00:13:25,429 --> 00:13:27,306
That's when you want to...

311
00:13:27,389 --> 00:13:29,975
take a machete from just...

312
00:13:30,142 --> 00:13:31,602
Just a quick beheading.

313
00:13:31,685 --> 00:13:32,978
[laughter]

314
00:13:34,313 --> 00:13:36,524
[Jerry] Okay, here's
an existential question.

315
00:13:36,607 --> 00:13:38,400
I don't prepare anything for these shows.

316
00:13:38,484 --> 00:13:39,318
No kidding.

317
00:13:39,693 --> 00:13:41,278
[both laugh]

318
00:13:43,739 --> 00:13:46,825
One of the greatest,
most satisfying laughs I ever got

319
00:13:47,243 --> 00:13:50,329
-is when I made Mel Brooks laugh.
-[Jerry] Wow.

320
00:13:51,247 --> 00:13:54,333
And I said to him, 
"What's your big beef with the Nazis?"

321
00:13:54,416 --> 00:13:55,251
[laughs]

322
00:13:55,334 --> 00:13:56,335
[laughing]

323
00:13:56,752 --> 00:13:57,586
And Mel said...

324
00:13:57,670 --> 00:13:59,296
What's my big beef?

325
00:14:02,341 --> 00:14:04,468
Like in the next seven nights 
of your life,

326
00:14:04,552 --> 00:14:06,971
do you have seven people
trying  to have dinner with you?

327
00:14:07,054 --> 00:14:11,350
You must be constantly schlepped
to every dinner, every group.

328
00:14:11,433 --> 00:14:15,271
Anybody's getting together,
"Call Marty." Right?

329
00:14:15,354 --> 00:14:17,982
-I tend to go with the same group.
-You do?

330
00:14:18,065 --> 00:14:20,776
-I'm very social. Absolutely, I'm social.
-Right.

331
00:14:20,860 --> 00:14:25,114
Did you have something
that you like to do that was fun

332
00:14:25,197 --> 00:14:27,283
that was outside of show business?

333
00:14:27,366 --> 00:14:28,826
Well, I told people I did.

334
00:14:30,202 --> 00:14:31,996
But, you know, and then people say,

335
00:14:32,079 --> 00:14:34,707
"Well, what do you do, Marty,
when you're not in show...?" Oh, tennis.

336
00:14:34,790 --> 00:14:36,250
-Love tennis.
-[Jerry laughs]

337
00:14:36,333 --> 00:14:37,918
-Couldn't care less about tennis.
-Really?

338
00:14:38,002 --> 00:14:41,547
-You would pretend to enjoy tennis?
-Just to make me sound more rounded.

339
00:14:41,630 --> 00:14:42,464
Right!

340
00:14:42,673 --> 00:14:43,924
[both laugh]

341
00:14:46,635 --> 00:14:50,306
Larry David, back to Larry,
is obsessed with his golf.

342
00:14:50,389 --> 00:14:52,266
-Yeah.
-And will talk about it.

343
00:14:52,933 --> 00:14:56,562
"You know, Kenny G told me
if I go one, two, three! Hold for a...."

344
00:14:56,645 --> 00:14:59,440
You know, that's... He'd practice that.
I'd say, "Larry, how'd you play?"

345
00:14:59,523 --> 00:15:01,942
"I played the worst [bleep] game 
I've ever played in my life."

346
00:15:02,026 --> 00:15:03,360
-He's never happy... 
-Never happy.

347
00:15:03,444 --> 00:15:04,486
...about how he did.

348
00:15:04,862 --> 00:15:08,407
You know? But it is a passion
that he loves and wants to improve on.

349
00:15:08,490 --> 00:15:10,159
-Oh, please.
-You don't buy it?

350
00:15:10,743 --> 00:15:12,036
That he's going to improve?

351
00:15:12,119 --> 00:15:13,913
[both laugh]

352
00:15:17,082 --> 00:15:19,209
You know, we didn't get back 
to Mike Nichols.

353
00:15:19,293 --> 00:15:20,544
This is how fast he was.

354
00:15:20,628 --> 00:15:22,755
One time, '94 or something,

355
00:15:22,963 --> 00:15:26,967
and we're at a gallery opening ,
and Mike said, "Where are you staying?"

356
00:15:27,551 --> 00:15:30,262
And I said, "The Essex House."
He said, "No, but really."

357
00:15:30,346 --> 00:15:31,639
[laughing]

358
00:15:35,517 --> 00:15:38,020
-You are a Jewish person, correct?
-Yeah, that's correct.

359
00:15:38,103 --> 00:15:39,063
Why is it...

360
00:15:40,022 --> 00:15:40,856
that...

361
00:15:41,774 --> 00:15:43,817
if you are born and raised Jewish...

362
00:15:43,901 --> 00:15:46,528
I love... I'm so excited right now.
I love being asked Jew questions.

363
00:15:46,612 --> 00:15:48,238
What's the funniest Jewish word?

364
00:15:48,781 --> 00:15:50,532
It might be schnorrer.

365
00:15:51,533 --> 00:15:53,118
-Do you know what it means?
-No.

366
00:15:53,202 --> 00:15:57,289
A schnorrer is someone who picks 
the cashews out of the mixed nuts.

367
00:15:57,373 --> 00:15:58,540
[both laughing]

368
00:16:03,420 --> 00:16:06,799
Oh, look at that cute little dog.
Hi, baby. Such a nice dog.

369
00:16:06,882 --> 00:16:09,218
-Hi, sweet dog.
-I would love a dog like that.

370
00:16:09,301 --> 00:16:10,427
Hi, little dog.

371
00:16:10,594 --> 00:16:12,554
You seem like a perfect dog.

372
00:16:12,805 --> 00:16:15,974
-A perfect dog. Yes! You're perfect.
-Here's the big question.

373
00:16:16,058 --> 00:16:18,352
Because you look like a dog, 
but you're not too big.

374
00:16:21,105 --> 00:16:22,898
I know you're not a car person. Just stop.

375
00:16:22,982 --> 00:16:24,817
-No, no, no. This is beautiful.
-It's beautiful.

376
00:16:25,192 --> 00:16:26,151
No, it's beautiful.

377
00:16:26,860 --> 00:16:28,570
Now, why not white walls?

378
00:16:29,947 --> 00:16:31,156
-Get in the car.
-All right.

379
00:16:35,369 --> 00:16:36,787
Santa Monica Car Wash.

380
00:16:36,870 --> 00:16:38,789
Tuesday special,  $17.99.

381
00:16:38,872 --> 00:16:40,040
Are you kidding me?

382
00:16:40,124 --> 00:16:42,876
It's $17.99 to get your car washed?

383
00:16:42,960 --> 00:16:44,461
You think that seems high?

384
00:16:45,337 --> 00:16:46,714
-Yeah.
-Oh, are you insane?

385
00:16:46,797 --> 00:16:49,550
You are living in a dream world 
of residuals.

386
00:16:52,636 --> 00:16:55,681
What if we looked at some stars' homes?

387
00:16:55,764 --> 00:16:58,475
-What if we get one of these maps?
-I'd love that. Okay, let's do it.

388
00:16:58,559 --> 00:17:00,728
I can't believe 
these people still do this.

389
00:17:03,063 --> 00:17:04,189
Do you recognize us?

390
00:17:04,523 --> 00:17:06,942
I... You guys are stars.

391
00:17:07,025 --> 00:17:08,652
Yeah. Yeah, kind of.

392
00:17:08,736 --> 00:17:09,570
It's on the house.

393
00:17:09,653 --> 00:17:11,155
-Hey, thanks.
-Oh, thank you very much.

394
00:17:11,238 --> 00:17:12,322
You're welcome. Have fun.

395
00:17:15,909 --> 00:17:18,787
Hey, how're you doing?
We're looking for stars, too!

396
00:17:19,413 --> 00:17:20,247
Yeah.

397
00:17:20,706 --> 00:17:22,791
Take a picture of me and Jerry
real quick.

398
00:17:25,586 --> 00:17:28,505
Now look at that house. 
Now that... Who lived there?

399
00:17:28,589 --> 00:17:29,840
Okay, that!

400
00:17:30,090 --> 00:17:31,341
[Martin] Oh, Lucille Ball!

401
00:17:31,425 --> 00:17:32,468
A friend of mine

402
00:17:32,968 --> 00:17:34,261
grew up in Beverly Hills

403
00:17:34,344 --> 00:17:37,514
and would... knew Lucie Junior.

404
00:17:37,598 --> 00:17:38,932
-The mom--
-Lucie Junior?

405
00:17:39,016 --> 00:17:40,392
Yeah, in other words, she--

406
00:17:40,768 --> 00:17:43,479
-You know, Lucie Arnaz.
-Yeah, but that's not a Junior.

407
00:17:44,480 --> 00:17:45,314
Why?

408
00:17:45,606 --> 00:17:46,607
She's female.

409
00:17:47,191 --> 00:17:48,025
[laughs]

410
00:17:53,030 --> 00:17:54,865
Oh, yeah, Jack Benny
lived around here, too.

411
00:17:54,948 --> 00:17:58,243
-Oh, they lived next door.
-Who do we love more than Jack Benny?

412
00:17:58,327 --> 00:18:00,204
-I love Jack Benny. No one.
-No one! 

413
00:18:00,287 --> 00:18:01,789
Oh, my God, that's exciting.

414
00:18:01,872 --> 00:18:03,373
This is so much fun.

415
00:18:10,005 --> 00:18:12,257
-Want to go to his house?
-Gotta go to Stan Laurel's, yeah.

416
00:18:12,341 --> 00:18:14,051
-Yeah, let's go to his house.
-"Hey, Ollie."

417
00:18:16,595 --> 00:18:19,223
Just the joy of hanging with these people.

418
00:18:19,306 --> 00:18:21,141
-That's the other thing.
-Yes. Yes.

419
00:18:21,225 --> 00:18:23,727
I mean, I think the hang
is more important sometimes than the job.

420
00:18:26,271 --> 00:18:29,191
-I'd like to see Cary Grant's house.
-[Jerry] I would, too.

421
00:18:36,532 --> 00:18:39,284
I couldn't believe I found this.
I just had to buy it.

422
00:18:39,576 --> 00:18:40,410
[Martin laughs]

423
00:18:40,494 --> 00:18:41,912
Jiminy Glick!

